  "abstract_html": "<p>We determine the leading metric and concentration scales of Hermitian–Yang–Mills flow for locally free extensions <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">I</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> on a compact Kähler surface, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> are distinct line bundles of equal degree and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math> is a nonempty reduced finite scheme. Fix Hermitian–Einstein metrics on the line factors and their product as determinant. For every smooth initial metric with this determinant, the integrated squared norm <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> of the fixed inclusion satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x0007E;</mi><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> is the volume and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the quadratic form of the inclusion norm at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math> in orthonormal coordinates. On the radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msqrt><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, the actual metrics converge smoothly to an explicit charge-one anti-self-dual metric. The inclusion half-height radius and the half-defect-energy radius have this same leading value. The proof corrects a compact family of metrics orthogonally to its small connection eigenspace and compares the resulting trajectory with every actual flow. It also gives a global inclusion profile and quantitative convergence outside shrinking balls. For arbitrary finite defects we prove <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; if a defect is nonreduced, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>",
